Background Sync Jobs – Caption Persistence in Supabase
In the legacy design, image captions and file labels are ultimately stored differently in the database (JSON vs arrays), and the sync layer reflects that:
Image Caption Deferred Sync (setImageCaptionDeferred): This job runs after an image upload, to save a caption that was entered while the image was offline. It fetches the procedure’s current image_urls and captions from Supabase
github.com
. Then it finds which uploaded URL corresponds to the local image (matching on the filename)
github.com
. It then updates the captions JSON: adding the caption text under captions.image[matchedUrl] while preserving the rest (including leaving captions.file unchanged)
github.com
. This updated captions object is written back to the procedures table
github.com
. After this, the image’s caption is permanently stored in the procedure’s JSON data on Supabase. (The code explicitly carries along captions.file in this update, but since it’s not used for any new data here, it’s just persisted as-is
github.com
.)
UploadProcedureImage Executor: When an image is uploaded, the job uses the provided captions state to perform a memory patch similar to files. If a caption existed for the local image URI, it transfers that caption to the new public URL key in the captions.image map in state
github.com
. (There’s no DB write here for captions – the actual DB update for captions is handled by the deferred job above once the URL is known. The immediate DB update is only for the image_urls list.)
No Equivalent for File Captions: For file attachments, there is no “setFileCaptionDeferred” job or similar. The label given to a file is saved directly as part of the file_labels array in the procedure record when the file is uploaded
github.com
. There is no separate JSON map for file captions to update, so the sync system does not need a second step for files. This asymmetry is exactly what the migration to attachment_captions aims to eliminate. With a unified table, adding or editing a caption/label for a file would likely mirror the image caption flow: each attachment (image or file) could have an entry in the attachment_captions table that can be updated independently of the procedure record. The new system would remove the need for the file_labels array and possibly the captions JSON on the procedure altogether, centralizing caption text in one relational table.
